{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Final Project and Professional Vision","title":"Professional Teaching Philosophy With AI","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting professional teaching philosophy with ai to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"02","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/06/01"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Professional Teaching Philosophy With AI\n\n![Professional Teaching Philosophy With AI textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/06-02-professional-teaching-philosophy-with-ai.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA teaching philosophy is the document a hiring committee, a mentor, or your future self reads to learn what you will actually do when a class is hard and a tool is tempting. Most candidate philosophies fail in the same way: they state warm beliefs (\"technology should empower every learner\") that no one could disagree with and that commit the writer to nothing. With AI now in nearly every classroom workflow, a philosophy that only emits values is worse than empty; it lets a teacher believe they have a stance while the actual practice (auto-grading, auto-feedback, data sharing) drifts wherever the default settings push it.\n\nThis section treats a teaching philosophy with AI as a set of operationalized commitments: for each value you claim, a specific practice that delivers it, a boundary you will refuse to cross, and a disclosure you owe students. The Design Tension Studio map is the proving ground, because a philosophy is only credible when its stated values survive contact with a real classroom conflict.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nTwo candidates write the same opening line: \"I believe AI should support, not replace, my professional judgment.\" Candidate A stops there. Candidate B continues: \"Concretely, AI drafts feedback on student writing, but I read every draft and revise it before it reaches a student, and AI never enters or changes a grade without my sign-off.\" Only Candidate B has said anything a committee can test against her practice, disagree with, or hold her to. The first is a sentiment; the second is a commitment with a visible boundary.\n\nThe difference is not eloquence. It is whether the philosophy specifies a behavior under pressure. The pressure case for AI is always the same: the tool offers to do something faster than you can, and the question is what you will refuse to hand over even when handing it over would be convenient.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA defensible AI teaching philosophy is built from value-to-practice triples, each of which makes an abstract belief falsifiable:\n\n- **The value** names what you are committed to (student autonomy, equity, accountability for grades).\n- **The enacted practice** names the observable behavior that delivers the value (students may opt for teacher-only feedback; you read AI feedback before it is sent).\n- **The boundary** names what you will refuse even when it costs you time (no AI-assigned grades, no uploading student work to a tool that trains on it).\n\nTwo further commitments turn a private stance into a professional one:\n\n- **Disclosure.** Students and families are told when AI has read or processed student work, because consent without notice is not consent.\n- **Consistency.** The values section and the practice section cannot contradict each other. A philosophy that claims autonomy while the described workflow auto-processes every submission is internally incoherent, and a reader will catch it.\n\n**Legal anchor.** The disclosure and data-protection boundaries rest on a statutory floor, not just professional courtesy. FERPA keeps education records under the school's direct control: the \"school official\" exception lets a vendor process student records only under contractual control, with data-use limits and no onward sharing or repurposing such as model training. COPPA requires verifiable parental consent before collecting data from children under 13, and the FTC's amended rule (finalized January 2025, with compliance required by April 2026) now requires separate consent before a child's data is disclosed to third parties and tightens retention and monetization limits. As of 2026, roughly half of US state education departments have issued generative-AI guidance for K-12. Treat these as the legal floor your commitments rest on; the ethical bar in your philosophy should sit above it.\n\nThe course anchors keep the triples honest. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) checks that the AI practice serves a real learning demand rather than novelty. Critical edtech studies (Selwyn) checks who carries the hidden cost of a convenient default. Datafication checks whether your disclosure actually matches what the tool does with the data. A value you cannot pair with a practice, a boundary, and a disclosure is a slogan, not a commitment.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A philosophy states: 'I value student autonomy,' and its workflow auto-processes every submission through an AI grader the moment it is uploaded. What is the defect?\" explanation=\"The values section and the practice section contradict each other. Claiming autonomy while the described workflow auto-processes every submission is internally incoherent, and a reader will catch it. The fix is an enacted practice (an opt-out) that actually delivers the claimed value.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It lacks eloquence\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The stated value and the described practice contradict each other\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It cites too few anchors\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It discloses too much to students\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nMatch each value to the observable practice that would make it a real commitment rather than a slogan:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Accountability for grades\",\"definition\":\"Open every AI-suggested score, check it against the rubric, and confirm or override before any grade posts\"},{\"term\":\"Student autonomy\",\"definition\":\"Tell students when AI has read their work and let them opt for teacher-only feedback\"},{\"term\":\"Data protection\",\"definition\":\"Refuse any tool that retains student essays or trains on submissions\"},{\"term\":\"Instructional purpose ([TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge))\",\"definition\":\"Use the AI feature only where it serves the unit learning target, not novelty\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies** (Selwyn), and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe improvement is not longer wording. It is a better trace. The reader can now see the artifact, the constraint from **critical edtech studies** (Selwyn), and the exact revision that followed.\n\n### Worked Example: An Annotated Philosophy Revision\n\nWatch a teaching-philosophy paragraph move from slogan to defensible stance.\n\n1. **Before (slogan).** \"I believe AI should empower every learner to reach their full potential.\" A reviewer annotates: \"empower how, with what limit, and what happens when it does not?\"\n2. **Name a committed practice.** Revision adds: \"I will use AI to generate first-draft feedback on student writing so I can spend conference time on each student's specific next step.\"\n3. **State the boundary.** \"I will not let AI assign or change a grade without my review, because grading is a judgment I am accountable for.\"\n4. **Disclose to students.** \"Students will be told when AI has read their work, and may opt for teacher-only feedback, because autonomy means they choose how their writing is processed.\"\n5. **Check consistency (after).** The values section now matches the practice section: autonomy is stated and the opt-out practice delivers it. The earlier slogan claimed autonomy while the draft practice had AI auto-correct work before submission, which the revision removed.\n\nThe before could fit any candidate; the after states what this teacher will do, will refuse, and will disclose, so a hiring committee can question it.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The tempting shortcut on a heavy grading week\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your philosophy claims: I am accountable for every grade. It is finals week, you have 120 essays, and the LMS offers a one-click setting: AI scores and auto-releases grades to students instantly. Choosing it would save you an entire evening. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Enable auto-release just this once; the deadline is real\",\"next\":\"release\",\"feedback\":\"A boundary that only holds when it is convenient is not a boundary. The pressure case is exactly where the philosophy is tested.\"},{\"label\":\"Let AI suggest scores but open each one, check it against the rubric, and confirm before release\",\"next\":\"review\",\"feedback\":\"You enacted the value with a practice and held the boundary under time pressure, which is what makes the commitment real.\"},{\"label\":\"Refuse all AI involvement and grade everything by hand\",\"next\":\"refuse\",\"feedback\":\"Honoring the value, but you may be refusing a legitimate efficiency. The boundary is on auto-release, not on AI assistance.\"}]},\"release\":{\"prompt\":\"A student appeals a grade. The auto-released score was wrong, and you never saw it before it posted. The student asks who actually graded the essay. What does this expose?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"My stated value was decorative; the gradebook contradicted the prose\",\"next\":\"incoherent\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The reader trusts the practice, not the prose, and concludes the accountability claim was not held.\"},{\"label\":\"Reinstate review-before-release as a non-negotiable boundary\",\"next\":\"review\",\"feedback\":\"Correcting course: the boundary must hold under pressure to be real.\"}]},\"refuse\":{\"prompt\":\"Grading by hand, you run out of time and give thin feedback to the last 30 students. Was a total refusal the only way to honor accountability?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No: AI may draft, but I review every score against the rubric before it posts\",\"next\":\"review\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. The boundary protects the value without discarding a legitimate practice.\"}]},\"review\":{\"prompt\":\"You review each AI-suggested score. You notice the AI is mostly scoring grammar, but your rubric rewards argument quality. What boundary does [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) now add?\",\"feedback\":\"[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) asks whether the AI score even measures the learning target. Since it rewards the wrong dimension, your practice needs a guardrail: you weight the rubric dimensions yourself rather than trusting the AI composite. The finished commitment is a procedure a mentor could audit on any assignment: AI suggests, you verify against the rubric, you control the final score, and no grade posts without your sign-off.\",\"choices\":[]},\"incoherent\":{\"prompt\":\"You rewrite the philosophy so the practice section matches the value section. What is the closing test?\",\"feedback\":\"Run the triple test: state the practice that enacts accountability (review every suggested score), the boundary that protects it (no auto-release), and the disclosure it requires (students are told AI drafts the first-pass score and you make the final call). A value you will not staff with a procedure is one you do not really hold.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you write any belief into a philosophy, run it through the triple test: state the practice that enacts it, the boundary that protects it, and the disclosure it requires. If a belief cannot generate all three, it is not yet a professional commitment and should be cut or rewritten.\n\nA philosophy paragraph should survive three reader questions:\n\n1. **Practice check:** What specific, observable thing will I do that enacts this value?\n2. **Boundary check:** What will I refuse to let AI do here, even when refusing costs me time?\n3. **Consistency check:** Does any practice I describe contradict a value I claim?\n\nIf any answer is missing or contradictory, the paragraph is a slogan and a committee will read it as one.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A peer writes: 'I will be careful and thoughtful with AI in my classroom.' Explain why a hiring committee reads this as a temperament rather than a commitment, and rewrite it into one value-practice-boundary-disclosure triple.\" answer=\"Carefulness names a disposition, not a checkable behavior: it has no boundary a reader can verify and no practice a mentor could observe, so it commits the writer to nothing. A committee cannot test 'careful' against the gradebook. A real triple makes the value falsifiable, for example: 'I am accountable for grades (value); AI may suggest a score and rationale, but I open every suggestion, check it against the rubric, and confirm or override before posting (practice); I will not let any grade auto-release without my review, even under deadline pressure (boundary); and students are told AI drafts the first-pass score while I make the final call, so an appealing student knows whom they are appealing to (disclosure).'\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai\",\"cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA frequent mistake is mistaking caution for a philosophy. \"I will be careful with AI\" names a temperament, not a commitment; carefulness has no boundary a reader can check. The opposite error is enthusiasm as substitute for judgment: \"I embrace AI to transform learning\" tells a committee nothing about what you would refuse.\n\nThe deeper trap is letting the philosophy and the gradebook disagree. A teacher can sincerely claim accountability for grades while quietly letting an LMS auto-score and auto-release. The reader who notices the contradiction trusts the practice, not the prose, and concludes the stated value is decorative.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake the value \"I am accountable for every grade a student receives\" and build it into a defensible commitment. The practice that enacts it: AI may suggest a score and a rationale, but you open every suggestion, check it against the rubric, and either confirm or override before any grade posts. The boundary that protects it: no automatic grade release, no AI changing a grade after a regrade request without your review. The disclosure it requires: students are told AI drafts the first-pass score and that you make the final call, so a student appealing a grade knows whom they are appealing to.\n\nNow stress-test the commitment against [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) and datafication. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) asks whether the AI score even measures the learning target; if the rubric rewards argument quality and the AI mostly scores grammar, the practice needs a guardrail (you weight the rubric dimensions yourself). Datafication asks whether the scoring tool retains the student essays; if it does, your accountability boundary now includes refusing tools that keep the work. The finished paragraph is no longer a belief about accountability; it is a procedure a mentor could audit on any given assignment.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a philosophy (yours or a peer's), underline every value claim and write next to it the practice, boundary, and disclosure that would make it real. Empty claims will have blank margins; those are the lines to rewrite. By the end of CAT 531 06.02 you should be able to read any teaching-philosophy paragraph and say within seconds whether it commits the writer to a checkable behavior or merely signals good intentions.\n\nA strong reader of an AI teaching philosophy can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each stated value, what observable practice enacts it?\n2. What is the boundary the writer will refuse to cross, even under time pressure?\n3. Does any described practice contradict a claimed value?\n4. What is disclosed to students, and does the disclosure match what the tools actually do?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA philosophy is strong when a skeptical reader can convert every paragraph into a behavior they could observe in your classroom next week. Eloquence is the last layer; the load-bearing layers are the value-practice-boundary triples and the consistency between what you claim and what you describe doing. If a paragraph has a value but no enacted practice, mark it and ask BigAL to compare a slogan version with a committed version of that exact belief rather than to \"make it stronger.\"\n\nA useful closing line would read: \"I claim accountability for grades and enact it by reviewing every AI-suggested score against the rubric before release; the practice I refused is auto-posting, because a value I will not staff with a procedure is one I do not really hold.\"\n\n**When the value meets a biased tool.** Suppose your disclosed value is \"AI gives every student fast feedback,\" but the auto-feedback or detection tool performs measurably worse on multilingual or AAVE writers (Liang et al., 2023, documented exactly this disparate false-positive pattern for non-native English writers). The value as stated silently shifts burden onto those students, which forces a boundary: \"I check tool error rates by subgroup before trusting AI feedback.\" A disclosed value without that boundary is a promise the tool cannot keep equitably.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same philosophy from a writing classroom to a math classroom. The value (accountability for grades) is stable, but the enacting practice must change: AI in math may auto-check computation reliably, so the boundary shifts from \"review every score\" to \"review every score where partial credit or reasoning is involved.\" If your philosophy only worked because you memorized the writing-class procedure, it will fail to transfer. If you understood the value-practice-boundary structure, you regenerate a new, defensible practice for the new subject without abandoning the value. That regeneration, not the original wording, is the evidence you actually hold the commitment.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/06-02-professional-teaching-philosophy-with-ai-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"06.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 06.02. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge. Teachers College Record. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. Sustainability, 16(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). Education and technology: Key issues and debates. Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2020). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. Learning, Media and Technology. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- Williamson, B., Macgilchrist, F., & Potter, J. (2023). Re-examining AI, automation and datafication in education. Learning, Media and Technology. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2023.2167830\n- Federal Trade Commission (2025). Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule: Final rule (16 CFR Part 312). https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/01/ftc-finalizes-changes-childrens-privacy-rule-limiting-companies-ability-monetize-kids-data\n- U.S. Department of Education. FERPA and the \"school official\" exception (34 CFR 99.31). https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai","stem":"A teaching-philosophy statement says: \"I believe AI should empower every learner.\" Which revision turns this into a defensible professional stance rather than a slogan?","options":["\"I believe AI is the future of education and embrace it fully.\"","\"I will use AI tools for formative feedback but keep grading and disclosure decisions with the teacher, because students must know when their work is read by a model.\"","\"I believe AI should empower and inspire every single learner equally.\"","\"I am cautiously optimistic about AI and will see how it goes.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A professional philosophy commits to a specific stance with a named boundary and a reason, not an aspirational slogan.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_slogan_as_stance","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai","stem":"A reviewer annotates a philosophy statement: \"Your values section says you prioritize student autonomy, but your practice section has the AI auto-correct student answers before they submit. These contradict.\" What does this note ask the writer to fix?","options":["Delete the practice section so only the stated values remain.","Add a citation to an autonomy theorist next to the values claim.","Align the stated value with an actual classroom practice, or revise the value so the philosophy is internally consistent.","Make the autonomy sentence longer and more confident."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A professional philosophy must show consistency between stated values and the practices the teacher actually commits to.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_values_practice_mismatch","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A candidate asks BigAL to write their entire teaching-philosophy statement and submits it unchanged. Beyond academic-honesty issues, why does this most undermine the artifact's purpose?","options":["The model writes too formally for a philosophy statement.","A teaching philosophy is supposed to disclose the author's own committed values and judgment; outsourcing it leaves no actual professional stance to defend in an interview or classroom.","The statement would be too short to meet the length requirement.","AI text always contains factual errors about education law."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The artifact's whole purpose is to make the teacher's own values inspectable; a generated stance cannot be defended as the teacher's judgment.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_outsourced_values","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai","stem":"Which philosophy statement would best survive a skeptical hiring committee asking, \"What would you NOT do with AI, even if it saved time?\"","options":["One that lists every AI tool the candidate has tried.","One that says AI should always be used to maximize efficiency.","One that names a specific line the teacher will not cross (for example, never letting AI assign final grades without human review) and gives the value that draws the line.","One that avoids mentioning AI to stay neutral."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A defensible philosophy names its limits, not only its enthusiasms; a stated boundary with a reason shows committed judgment.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_no_stated_limits","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai","evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_slogan_as_stance","pattern":"aspirational_slogan_as_philosophy","description":"Writing inspirational slogans about AI instead of a committed, bounded professional stance","trigger":"philosophy statement uses words like empower or transform with no named practice or limit","feedback":"Replace the slogan with a specific commitment, the boundary it implies, and the reason behind it.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_values_practice_mismatch","pattern":"stated_value_contradicts_practice","description":"Stating a value the described AI practice actually violates","trigger":"values section and practice section of the statement contradict each other","feedback":"Align the value with a real practice or revise the value so the philosophy is internally consistent.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_outsourced_values","pattern":"philosophy_outsourced_to_ai","description":"Letting AI author the teacher's own values rather than using it to pressure-test a stance the teacher already holds","trigger":"statement of personal values is generated wholesale and submitted unchanged","feedback":"Write your own committed stance first; use AI only to test it for gaps or contradictions you must judge.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_2_professional_teaching_philosophy_with_ai_no_stated_limits","pattern":"philosophy_without_limits","description":"Listing only enthusiasms for AI without naming any line the teacher will not cross","trigger":"statement names benefits and tools but no refusal or boundary","feedback":"Name at least one thing you will not do with AI and the value that draws that line.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d63115165924b8e7314162dc369da771aa34a9b96a0206bfed98e5ba46f4cc22","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}